{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Serve the inventory application on node1 over TCP port 8181.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Lighttpd listened on 0.0.0.0:8181 and returned the inventory page, health response, and JSON data through node1's managed IP. The listener, health response, and identical inventory data were observed again after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-edbe74a96426417c","cmd-36f6fbbb54354536","cmd-25a69edcdfd14a6e","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Keep mandatory access control in enforcing mode while the inventory application operates.","status":"satisfied","summary":"AppArmor was enabled with the inventory-httpd profile and Lighttpd process in enforce mode while the application was healthy. A forbidden /etc/shadow read was denied and audited. Enforcement, confinement, listener, and health all recovered after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e23dbb0fd3924a7f","cmd-442b2d5b7e1d4574","cmd-404f0b59460a4dd0","cmd-36f6fbbb54354536","cmd-25a69edcdfd14a6e"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Retain the installed operating-system release and kernel, using software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The node remained on Alpine Linux 3.23.3 and kernel 6.18.7-0-lts from baseline through the final post-restart state. AppArmor 4.1.0-r4 and Lighttpd 1.4.85-r0 were installed from the configured Alpine v3.23 repositories and operated successfully after restart.","evidence_ids":["global:before_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","cmd-7eb0c3ad633a4f7d","cmd-50270cf440434307","cmd-370b453282164fc5","cmd-25a69edcdfd14a6e"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.88,"summary":"The final state is healthy and package consistency shows no new damage. Two low-risk implementation experiments were unnecessary: cmd-779ccaa20c7e4fa8 created an invalid /usr/sbin/httpd profile and service definition, later overwritten, while its application files and boot-argument changes contributed to the solution; cmd-d6abfae225a6462b created a BusyBox-httpd symlink and corresponding profile/service even though the httpd applet was unavailable. The symlink was removed and the profile/service replaced by cmd-bb221488e4664ed5, although the evidence does not establish whether the empty helper directory remained. Two reboot log files were left under /tmp after otherwise necessary reboots rather than cleaned up. No conflicting listener, failed final service, unsafe package-source change, or package damage was observed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-779ccaa20c7e4fa8","cmd-d6abfae225a6462b","cmd-bb221488e4664ed5","cmd-9c389f98020b436a","cmd-136f1e7b39c54e4b","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. The independent restart protocol is also complete: node1 had a healthy pre-restart state, explicit restart acknowledgement, a changed boot identity, and recovered AppArmor enforcement, service, listener, health, and matching data. The lifecycle boot observations independently corroborate the restart.","limitations":["The lifecycle failed-service probe was unsupported because it checks systemd on an OpenRC system; direct OpenRC service checks nevertheless showed the inventory service started before and after restart.","The trace does not show whether the empty /usr/local/libexec/inventory directory from the abandoned BusyBox experiment remained after its symlink was removed."]}
